@@ -2679,6 +2679,37 @@ Note that the unencapsulated function may be @code{eq} to the designated
 function even in the presence of encapsulations. For generic
 functions, this is currently always the case.
 @end deffn
+@anchor{Function sb-ext parse-float}
+@ffindex @sortas{parse-float sb-ext} parse-float [sb-ext]
+@deffn{Function} sb-ext:parse-float string &key start end junk-allowed
+Parses a decimal floating-point number in the substring of @code{string}
+delimited by @code{start} and @code{end}, skipping leading and trailing
+whitespace, like @code{parse-integer}. Returns two values: the float, and
+the index where parsing stopped.
+
+The syntax is that of floats in the reader: an optional sign, digits with
+an optional decimal point (at least one digit), and an optional exponent
+consisting of a marker (@code{e}, @code{s}, @code{f}, @code{d} or
+@code{l}, in either case), an optional sign and digits. Integers such as
+@code{"12"} and @code{"12."} are also accepted and give floats. The
+exponent marker decides the float format as in the reader; without one,
+or with @code{e}, it is @code{*read-default-float-format*}. The result is
+correctly rounded and equal to what @code{read-from-string} returns for
+the same float syntax.
+
+Signals a @code{parse-error} if the substring is not a float, unless
+@code{junk-allowed} is true, in which case it returns @code{nil} if there
+is no number and stops at the first character that is not part of one.
+Also signals a @code{parse-error} if the number is too large for its
+float format.
+
+@example
+(sb-ext:parse-float "  2.5  ")              @result{} 2.5, 7
+(sb-ext:parse-float "1.5d300")              @result{} 1.5d300, 7
+(sb-ext:parse-float "12")                   @result{} 12.0, 2
+(sb-ext:parse-float "3.25 kg" :junk-allowed t) @result{} 3.25, 4
+@end example
+@end deffn
